<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ba'ath Party</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1947">1947</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Arab Socialist Ba'ath Party</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ba'ath Party, or Arab Socialist Ba'ath Party, was a pan-Arab nationalist and socialist party founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> in 1947 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michel-aflaq">Michel Aflaq</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Salah al-Din</a> al-Bitar. It split into rival Syrian and Iraqi wings after 1966. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> it briefly took power in 1963, returned in 1968 and ruled until the US-led invasion of 2003; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> it held power from 1963 until the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bashar-al-assad">Bashar al-Assad</a> in 2024.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/baath-party.jpg" alt="Ba'ath Party" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Drawn by Darz Mol. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Ba%27ath_Party.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From the coup of 8 March 1963 the <b>Arab Socialist Ba'ath Party</b> ruled Syria as a one-party police state.</blockquote><a href="/empire/syrian-arab-republic">Syrian Arab Republic · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Qasim left the Baghdad Pact, began land reform and claimed Kuwait, but was killed in a <b>Ba'athist</b> coup in 1963.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-iraq">Republic of Iraq</a></li><li><blockquote>Parliamentary rule then returned, with the <b>Ba'ath</b> party gaining influence.</blockquote><a href="/state/syrian-republic">Syrian Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>Syrian officers and <b>Ba'athist</b> politicians, alarmed by communist influence and inspired by Nasser's prestige after the 1956 Suez crisis, asked Egypt for union.</blockquote><a href="/state/united-arab-republic">United Arab Republic</a></li></ul></section>
